Logic :

solved this problem using the backtracking technique.maintain a temporary list that represents the current permutation being built and a boolean array to track which elements have already been used.
At each recursive call,  iterate through all the numbers. If a number has not been used yet,  add it to the current permutation, mark it as visited, and recursively continue building the permutation.
When the size of the current list becomes equal to the size of the input array, it means it formed one complete permutation.  create a copy of the current list and add it to the final answer.
After returning from the recursive call, it perform backtracking by removing the last added element and marking it as unused. This restores the previous state so I can try the next available number at the current position.
By exploring every unused element at every position and backtracking after each choice, the algorithm systematically generates all possible permutations without duplicates (assuming the input contains distinct elements).


Code :

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans=new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> list=new ArrayList<>();
        boolean[] f=new boolean[nums.length];
        permutation(nums,f,list,ans);
        return ans;
    }
    void permutation(int[] nums,boolean[] f,List<Integer> list,List<List<Integer>> ans){
        if(list.size()==nums.length){
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list));
            return;
        }
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            if(!f[i]){
                list.add(nums[i]);
                f[i]=true;
                permutation(nums,f,list,ans);
                f[i]=false;
                list.remove(list.size()-1);
            }
        }
    }
}
